# OpenCovers — environment variables for local development.
#
#   cp .env.example .env     (then uncomment only what you need to override)
#
# Local development works with NO .env at all: every variable below has a default
# for localhost (BETTER_AUTH_SECRET's is public: fine locally, never elsewhere). The API loads ../../.env automatically when the file
# exists (see the `dev`, `start` and `db:migrate` scripts in apps/api).
#
# Every line is commented out on purpose. The code reads `process.env.X ?? default`,
# so an EMPTY value (`PORT=`) would override the default with an empty string.
# Uncomment a line only when you give it a real value. Never commit a real .env.
#
# For the production Docker stack use .env.prod.example instead (compose.prod.yaml).

# --- API (apps/api) ----------------------------------------------------------

# PostgreSQL connection string for the dev database.
# Default: postgres://postgres:postgres@localhost:5432/opencovers
# Comes from `docker compose up -d` (compose.yaml) or your own PostgreSQL 16.
# DATABASE_URL=

# Port the API listens on. Default: 3000.
# The staff app dev server proxies /api and /v1 to http://localhost:3000.
# PORT=

# Secret that signs sessions (Better Auth). Default: a fixed development value that
# is public in the source, so anyone can forge sessions if it is left in place.
# REQUIRED in any shared or production environment. Generate: openssl rand -base64 32
# BETTER_AUTH_SECRET=

# Secret that keys the guest blocklist hashes (ADR-0018). Default: a fixed development
# value that is public in the source. REQUIRED in any shared or production environment,
# separate from BETTER_AUTH_SECRET. Base64 of at least 32 bytes: openssl rand -base64 32.
# Any other value stops the API at startup. Never store it with a database dump: a dump
# restored under another key leaves the list inactive.
# BLOCKLIST_KEY_PREVIOUS holds the old key during a rotation, so entries made under it
# still match. Remove it once no entry under the old key remains.
# BLOCKLIST_KEY=
# BLOCKLIST_KEY_PREVIOUS=

# Who may create an account: open, first-user or closed. Default: open, so local
# development needs nothing. An unknown value stops the API at startup.
# first-user lets the first account sign up and refuses every later one. closed refuses all.
# compose.prod.yaml defaults to first-user. See docs/adr/0005-signup-control.md.
# Under first-user and closed only the owner (the first account) can create venues.
# See docs/adr/0008-venue-creation-limit.md.
# SIGNUP=

# Rate limits on sign-in, sign-up, invitation redemption and venue creation. See docs/adr/0007-rate-limiting.md.
# RATE_LIMIT is on or off. Default: on when NODE_ENV=production, off otherwise, so local development
# needs nothing. RATE_LIMIT=on or off always wins. Outside Docker, set NODE_ENV=production or RATE_LIMIT=on.
# TRUST_PROXY is true or false. Default: false, which uses the socket address and ignores
# X-Forwarded-For. Set true only when a reverse proxy is the sole way to reach the API.
# The four budgets read max/window, with a window in s, m or h. A bad value stops the API at startup.
#   RATE_LIMIT_AUTH        default 30/15m   sign-in and other writes under /api/auth
#   RATE_LIMIT_SIGNUP      default 5/1h     sign-up
#   RATE_LIMIT_INVITATION  default 10/15m   invitation redemption and sign-up with an invitation token
#   RATE_LIMIT_VENUE_CREATION default 10/1h  POST /v1/venues
# The guest booking routes under /v1/public have five more, GET included.
# See docs/adr/0017-booking-widget-and-self-service-link.md.
#   RATE_LIMIT_PUBLIC_READ         default 300/15m  every GET under /v1/public, per address
#   RATE_LIMIT_PUBLIC_BOOKING      default 10/1h    public creates and link resends, per address
#   RATE_LIMIT_PUBLIC_VENUE        default 100/1h   the same routes, per booking key (all addresses together)
#   RATE_LIMIT_PUBLIC_SELF_SERVICE default 60/15m   every /v1/public/self-service request, per address
#   RATE_LIMIT_PUBLIC_DAYS         default 60/15m   GET /v1/public/{bookingKey}/days, per address (also spends PUBLIC_READ)
# RATE_LIMIT=
# TRUST_PROXY=
# RATE_LIMIT_AUTH=
# RATE_LIMIT_SIGNUP=
# RATE_LIMIT_INVITATION=
# RATE_LIMIT_VENUE_CREATION=
# RATE_LIMIT_PUBLIC_READ=
# RATE_LIMIT_PUBLIC_BOOKING=
# RATE_LIMIT_PUBLIC_VENUE=
# RATE_LIMIT_PUBLIC_SELF_SERVICE=
# RATE_LIMIT_PUBLIC_DAYS=

# Bot protection on the public booking create (ADR-0017). Every create sends back a signed form
# challenge from the widget config and a proof of work for it.
# PUBLIC_POW_DIFFICULTY is the number of leading zero bits, 0 to 32. Default: 12, a fraction of a
# second in a browser. 0 turns the proof of work off (the signature and the 3-second minimum stay),
# which is handy for local development and scripts. A bad value stops the API at startup.
# The API logs the effective settings at startup, and a warning under NODE_ENV=production when the
# difficulty is 0 or PUBLIC_CHALLENGE_SECRET is unset.
# PUBLIC_CHALLENGE_SECRET signs the challenges, at least 32 characters. Default: a random key per
# process, so a restart voids open challenges. Set it when more than one API process serves the widget.
# PUBLIC_POW_DIFFICULTY=
# PUBLIC_CHALLENGE_SECRET=

# The guest booking widget (ADR-0017). Locally there is nothing to set: `pnpm dev` rebuilds apps/widget,
# the staff app's dev server proxies /w/* to the API, and the widget opens at
# http://localhost:5173/w/<bookingKey>.
# WIDGET_ADDRESS is the widget's own host in production, such as book.example.com. Set, the API serves the
# widget page only for requests to that host. Unset, the API serves it on any host, except with
# NODE_ENV=production, where it serves none. A host name shared with BETTER_AUTH_URL or TRUSTED_ORIGINS
# stops the API at startup. It is also the origin of the links a lost-link resend emails. Unset outside
# production, those links use BETTER_AUTH_URL.
# WIDGET_DIST is the widget build the API serves. Default: apps/widget/dist.
# WIDGET_ADDRESS=
# WIDGET_DIST=

# Public URL of the API as users reach it. Default: http://localhost:3000
# BETTER_AUTH_URL=

# Comma-separated browser origins allowed to sign in / call the API. A write made with the session
# cookie from any other Origin fails with 403.
# Default: the local dev origins. Set it to your public URL when you deploy.
# TRUSTED_ORIGINS=

# Email confirmations through Resend. Optional: with these unset, no email is sent.
# RESEND_API_KEY is created at https://resend.com/api-keys (a secret).
# RESEND_FROM_EMAIL is a sender on a domain you verified in Resend.
# RESEND_API_KEY=
# RESEND_FROM_EMAIL=

# --- Hosted demo (apps/api, ADR-0022). Leave all of these unset on a normal instance. ---

# 1 turns the API into the public demo: magic-link and Google sign-in, one sandbox restaurant per visitor,
# password sign-up off. 0 or unset: off. Any other value stops the API at startup. Run it on its own database.
# With NODE_ENV=production it needs RESEND_API_KEY and RESEND_FROM_EMAIL (above) to send the magic links.
# Without them the link is printed to the API log, which is enough for a local trial.
# DEMO_MODE=

# Google sign-in for the demo (a Google OAuth client, redirect URI BETTER_AUTH_URL/api/auth/callback/google).
# Set both or neither. The Google account must report a verified email.
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=

# Most reservations and guests one sandbox may hold. Defaults: 150 and 100.
# DEMO_MAX_RESERVATIONS=
# DEMO_MAX_GUESTS=

# Magic link limits, as max/window like the other rate limits (ADR-0022, decision 8a).
# Per address per minute (default 1/1m), per address per day (5/24h), per IP per hour (10/1h),
# and challenge requests per IP per hour (60/1h).
# RATE_LIMIT_MAGIC_LINK=
# RATE_LIMIT_MAGIC_LINK_DAY=
# RATE_LIMIT_MAGIC_LINK_IP=
# RATE_LIMIT_DEMO_CHALLENGE=

# `pnpm demo:cleanup` deletes sandboxes unused for 14 days (run it daily). `pnpm demo:leads` prints consented leads as CSV.

# --- Tests (apps/api) --------------------------------------------------------

# Database the integration tests wipe and use. Default:
# postgres://postgres:postgres@localhost:5432/opencovers_test
# Never point it at data you care about. compose.yaml creates opencovers_test.
# TEST_DATABASE_URL=

# Development switch for webhooks (apps/api/src/lib/webhooks.ts). Only the exact
# string `true` turns it on; then webhooks may target plain http and private
# addresses, and the API logs a warning at startup. The webhook e2e test sets it.
# Never enable it in a shared or production environment.
# WEBHOOK_ALLOW_INSECURE=

# --- MCP server (apps/mcp) ---------------------------------------------------

# Base URL of the API the MCP server talks to. Default: http://localhost:3000
# OPENCOVERS_BASE_URL=

# Agent key the MCP server authenticates with (a secret). Create it in the staff
# app under Setup → API keys (venue admins only). It is usually set in the MCP
# host's config (Claude Desktop, Cursor), not here.
# OPENCOVERS_API_KEY=
